  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Doug Selwyn's <em>All Children Are All Our Children</em> (Peter Lang, 2019) functions as a pedagogical anchor within educational scholarship on child development and school community. The work has accumulated 2 citations to date, with emerging uptake in gifted education and postdigital pedagogy. It holds a documented presence in academic libraries and has been highlighted in educational video discourse, signaling early adoption within teaching contexts.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published in 2019 by Peter Lang in the Counterpoints series\u2014a long-running venue for critical education scholarship\u2014this book enters a field concerned with psychological development, school health, and community engagement. The work is openly available, widening its potential reach beyond subscription-restricted publishing models. Library catalog records indicate holdings in five institutions, positioning it within academic collections focused on school improvement, student health, and stress in educational contexts.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Scholarly uptake, though modest in absolute terms, shows thematic coherence. A 2020 formal review appeared in <em>Postdigital Science and Education</em>, offering early critical engagement. More substantially, a 2025 article in <em>Educational Psychologist</em>\u2014itself cited 23 times\u2014drew on Selwyn's framework when addressing culturally responsive approaches to twice-exceptional Black males in gifted education. This citation demonstrates the work's utility in advancing equity-centered discourse within gifted education research, a field increasingly concerned with identifying and supporting underrepresented students.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Beyond formal citations, the book appears in teaching contexts. A YouTube video featuring Selwyn discussing the work's themes has been recorded, providing visibility in the growing ecosystem of educational video content. The catalogue subjects\u2014school health services, community-school collaboration, student wellness, and stress management\u2014align with how educators and librarians categorize the work's relevance to lived classroom concerns.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">At five years post-publication, the citation record remains sparse but directional. The gap between 2020 and 2025 citations suggests the work may be in a secondary adoption phase, influencing practitioners and researchers without yet becoming a canonical reference. The institutional concentration of citing authors across multiple universities and the focus on gifted education and child health indicate that impact, where present, clusters within specific scholarly communities rather than spanning the broader education field.</p>",
